  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 104 AM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nHeadlines of heat and limited precipitation continue in the \nextended. Upper level high pressure will slide south on Wednesday \nand Thursday before finally transitioning west on Friday and the \nweekend. Next week is where the upper level pattern become \ninteresting, since long range guidance is not keen on keeping the \nridge anchored over the Four Corners region. Models suggest there \nwill be an elongation of the ridge across the CONUS heading into \nMonday. Zonal flow aloft appears possible if troughing becomes more \nrestricted. Beyond Day 7, models still have some uncertainty on the \nplacement of high pressure, but there are some hints that it could \nset up over the eastern CONUS.\n\nHigh temperatures should still slowly degrade and bottom out in the \nmid to upper 90s this weekend. By next Monday, signals for more \nwidespread 100s return to the forecast, so relief from heat will \neither be temporary or non-existant. PoPs remain in play for the \nrest of the week. If northwest flow is to be cut off and replaced by \nzonal flow, much drier conditions can be expected for next week.\n\nRangel",
